+  networks, stacks, backups, vulnerabilities, files); it is a per-browser
+  convenience, nothing is stored in debcontrol.
+- **Fits half a screen and a phone.** The vulnerability counts are one
+  short line (critical and high as badges, the rest as a number; the
+  line links to the details). When the table is narrower than about
+  1000 px the counts move under the image instead of taking a column;
+  below about 700 px (a phone) every row of every Docker table turns into
+  a card with "column: value" lines.
+- *Start*, *Stop*, *Restart* (`action.power`) under each container's name
+  work as before (`POST /machines/{id}/containers/{name}/{action}`,
+  audited as `machine.container.<action>`).
 - *Check image updates* (`machine.manage`) runs the registry-digest
   comparison described on [Monitoring](Monitoring#docker-containers).
-- *Update selected* / *Update all with a newer image* (`action.updates`)
-  queue one update job per container; they run one after another.
+- *Update all with a newer image* (`action.updates`) queues one update
+  job per container; they run one after another.
+
+### Several containers at once
+
+Tick containers (or the box in the header for all of them) and use the
+buttons under the table. Each needs the same permission as doing it to
+one container:
+
+| Button | What it does | Permission |
+|---|---|---|
+| *Start* / *Restart* / *Stop* | one `docker start/restart/stop` for all the selected containers | `action.power` |
+| *Update selected* | one update job per container, as below | `action.updates` |
+| *Scan images* | one scan job of the selected containers' images | `action.updates` |
+| *Remove* | `docker rm` of the selected containers - **stopped ones only**: docker refuses a running one, so stop it first; volumes and images stay | `action.updates` |
+
+debcontrol's own containers have no checkbox, and are left out of
+everything but *Start* even when asked for over the API (audited as a
+refusal). Start/stop/restart/remove wait for docker's answer and report
+it, like a single container's buttons; each container gets its own audit
+entry (`machine.container.start` / `.stop` / `.restart` / `.remove`).
+
+### Links to a container's web interface
+
+The **Ports / links** column (and *Web* on a container's page) turns
+what the container declares into links that open in a new tab:
+
+| From | Becomes |
+|---|---|
+| a Traefik router label, ``traefik.http.routers.<name>.rule=Host(`app.example.com`)`` (with `PathPrefix`, if any) | `https://app.example.com` - plain `http` only when the router has no TLS and only `web`/`http` entrypoints; nothing when `traefik.enable=false` |
+| a caddy-docker-proxy label, `caddy=app.example.com` | `https://app.example.com` (or the `http://` address given) |
+| a published TCP port, `0.0.0.0:8080->80/tcp` | `http://<machine address>:8080` - `https` for ports 443 and 8443; a port bound to `127.0.0.1` only gets no link |
+| `debcontrol.url=https://grafana.example.com/d/home` | exactly that link (several, comma-separated); when present, the only links shown |
+| `debcontrol.https=9443,10443` | open these published ports with `https` |
+| `debcontrol.hide-ports=true` | no links for published ports |
+
+So for a service behind a proxy debcontrol can't see (for example Traefik
+on another host with a file provider), add `debcontrol.url` to its
+compose file:
+
+```yaml
+services:
+  grafana:
+    image: grafana/grafana:12
+    labels:
+      debcontrol.url: "https://grafana.example.com"
+```
+
+Labels are written by whoever controls the machine, so debcontrol only
+accepts `http`/`https` URLs without a user name or password and checks
+host names part by part; links open with `rel="noopener noreferrer"`,
+and debcontrol's server never fetches them.
+
+**Image links.** Next to a newer image version: *Registry* (the image's
+page on Docker Hub, GHCR or Quay) and, from the image's own
+`org.opencontainers.image.source` label, *Source* and *Release notes*
+(the `/releases` page on GitHub or Codeberg, `/-/releases` on GitLab) -
+so you can see what changed before updating. A container's page shows
+them always.
 
 **A container's page** (`/machines/{id}/docker/containers/{name}`) shows
 the container as `docker inspect` describes it right now - image, state,
